<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Hayato</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 720 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The people of southern Kyushu who resisted incorporation into the early Japanese state until their great rebellion was crushed in 720.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Hayato lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osumi-province">Osumi</a> regions of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and are often linked to the Kumaso of earlier legend. They appear in court records from the late 7th century as a frontier people paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yamato">Yamato court</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their society was organised in local groups under chiefs, and their customs, language and dress were considered foreign by the court. Groups of Hayato were brought to the capital to serve as guards and perform ritual dances and chants at court ceremonies. The imposition of ritsuryo provinces and land registers on their territory provoked resistance.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A major rebellion broke out in 720 and was suppressed after more than a year by an army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-no-tabito">Otomo no Tabito</a>. Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> was then integrated into the provincial system; Hayato tribute missions ended in 801, though Hayato still served at court ceremonies in the 9th century.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 600 CE – 720 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Korea and Japan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:71.64%;width:2.39%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 720 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kumaso, Hayahito</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=660&amp;focus=hayato" aria-label="Hayato on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8156 871 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hayato lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8441 1168h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8476 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8476 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Japan. <a href="/atlas?year=660&amp;focus=hayato">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

